1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,490
Microsoft has some of the most advanced AI

2
00:00:02,540 --> 00:00:03,710
in the world

3
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:06,310
So how did its email, its meetings and its

4
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:08,950
AI assistant go down for days?

5
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,650
In Britain, hospitals went back to paper

6
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:14,230
This week also brought the largest IPO in history

7
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:17,510
and Gulf investors made big paper gains from it

8
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:20,690
California is suing OpenAI over AI safety

9
00:00:20,740 --> 00:00:22,810
And Google and China's Alibaba are fighting

10
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:24,910
a price war on AI models

11
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:26,450
Let's get into it

12
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:28,630
Hi everyone, welcome to AI This Week

13
00:00:28,680 --> 00:00:30,050
I'm Wissam Kishek

14
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:32,250
I've spent 25 years in enterprise software

15
00:00:32,300 --> 00:00:35,470
across the Middle East, Türkiye and Africa

16
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:37,430
Let's start with the full story

17
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:40,370
On Friday, November 13th, Outlook email went

18
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:42,590
down, and so did Teams meetings

19
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:45,650
Even the AI assistant, Copilot, stopped working

20
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:48,370
and the outage lasted several days

21
00:00:48,420 --> 00:00:50,330
According to a detailed report from a site

22
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:53,110
that specializes in tech policy, Britain paid

23
00:00:53,160 --> 00:00:54,430
a heavy price

24
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:57,450
Almost every National Health Service body there lost email

25
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:00,670
Some medical staff went back to paper records

26
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:02,970
and appointments and operations were cancelled

27
00:01:03,020 --> 00:01:04,690
including C-sections

28
00:01:04,740 --> 00:01:07,350
Police couldn't take reports online, and courts

29
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:08,890
cancelled or postponed cases

30
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:12,610
And according to the same report, up to 99%

31
00:01:12,660 --> 00:01:16,170
of UK government staff use Microsoft to communicate

32
00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:18,190
A British minister told Parliament the incident

33
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:20,870
could have been more serious if it had lasted longer

34
00:01:20,920 --> 00:01:23,150
So, why did this happen?

35
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:26,330
Satya Nadella, Microsoft's CEO, spoke on Wednesday

36
00:01:26,380 --> 00:01:28,010
at a conference in New York

37
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:30,550
According to Anadolu, he described the outage

38
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:33,430
as a "perfect storm" that exposed a software

39
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:35,290
bug no one had caught before

40
00:01:35,340 --> 00:01:38,470
And he warned that AI could bring more outages

41
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:43,090
The reason: AI tools are now finding vulnerabilities very fast

42
00:01:43,140 --> 00:01:45,670
So companies rush to close them with updates

43
00:01:45,720 --> 00:01:48,250
with less time for testing, and a rushed update

44
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:49,910
can introduce new bugs

45
00:01:49,960 --> 00:01:53,370
He also said AI could become like "a nation-state-level

46
00:01:53,420 --> 00:01:55,730
attacker that's after you all the time." And

47
00:01:55,780 --> 00:01:57,570
here I want to give you my personal view

48
00:01:57,620 --> 00:01:59,610
This is opinion, not news

49
00:01:59,660 --> 00:02:02,030
I'm not convinced by this explanation

50
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:04,030
Companies like Microsoft were early in the

51
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:05,930
AI game, and they understand it

52
00:02:05,980 --> 00:02:07,790
They have access to models that are at least

53
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:09,670
two or three versions ahead of what the rest

54
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:10,830
of us use

55
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:12,710
Those models should be running on their own

56
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:15,810
systems and platforms, finding the bugs, writing

57
00:02:15,860 --> 00:02:18,370
the patches and retesting them, much faster

58
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:20,070
than any amateur out there

59
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:21,690
And there's a second point

60
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:24,250
A company the size of Microsoft builds in layers

61
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:27,710
of redundancy: backup systems, other regions

62
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:29,810
the ability to roll an update back

63
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:32,530
So for an outage to last several days is, to

64
00:02:32,580 --> 00:02:34,550
me, quite strange

65
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:36,290
Explaining it by saying people are now using

66
00:02:36,340 --> 00:02:37,890
AI doesn't convince me

67
00:02:37,940 --> 00:02:39,630
And who carries the cost of an outage like

68
00:02:39,680 --> 00:02:41,750
this is still an open question

69
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:44,190
In Britain, the outage opened up the debate

70
00:02:44,240 --> 00:02:45,990
on digital sovereignty

71
00:02:46,040 --> 00:02:48,530
The Liberal Democrats said no single provider

72
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:51,370
should have this much impact on health and policing

73
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:52,930
And they called on the government for a plan

74
00:02:52,980 --> 00:02:56,770
to reduce reliance on a few big American companies

75
00:02:56,820 --> 00:02:59,150
Britain still has no clear strategy for this

76
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:01,050
So, what does this mean for us?

77
00:03:01,100 --> 00:03:04,430
The outage was global, so this isn't just a British story

78
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:06,670
How many organizations here run all their work

79
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:09,230
on email and meetings from a single provider?

80
00:03:09,280 --> 00:03:11,490
The question for every manager: is there a

81
00:03:11,540 --> 00:03:13,290
plan if email goes down for two days?

82
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:15,830
And are updates tested before they roll out?

83
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:18,230
And the digital sovereignty question raised

84
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:20,610
in Britain applies the same way to any government

85
00:03:20,660 --> 00:03:23,470
that relies on a single foreign provider

86
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:25,710
Now let's head to the Gulf, because Gulf money

87
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:28,970
has become part of the AI story in America

88
00:03:29,020 --> 00:03:31,490
On Thursday, at the Abu Dhabi Investment Forum

89
00:03:31,540 --> 00:03:34,390
in New York, the UAE showcased its investments

90
00:03:34,440 --> 00:03:35,310
in the US

91
00:03:35,360 --> 00:03:37,310
And according to The National, the figure is

92
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:39,550
more than $1 trillion

93
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:41,450
And the two main sectors at the forum were

94
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:42,970
health and AI

95
00:03:43,020 --> 00:03:43,930
What's behind that number?

96
00:03:43,980 --> 00:03:47,130
Abu Dhabi investment firm MGX entered a partnership

97
00:03:47,180 --> 00:03:48,910
with BlackRock in July

98
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:52,010
The partnership is worth $40 billion, for digital

99
00:03:52,060 --> 00:03:53,070
infrastructure

100
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:55,270
And at the time, the same firm said it had

101
00:03:55,320 --> 00:03:58,510
raised $49 billion for its AI fund

102
00:03:58,560 --> 00:04:01,090
And it plans to put up to 80% of its portfolio

103
00:04:01,140 --> 00:04:03,450
into US projects

104
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:06,910
In Saudi Arabia, the story is more about people than money

105
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:10,170
According to the Saudi Press Agency, SDAIA

106
00:04:10,220 --> 00:04:13,090
the Saudi Data and AI Authority, launched a

107
00:04:13,140 --> 00:04:16,330
"Year of AI" initiative across universities

108
00:04:16,380 --> 00:04:18,290
And according to a report in the Saudi press

109
00:04:18,340 --> 00:04:21,250
HUMAIN is targeting the skills gap in Saudi Arabia

110
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:23,050
with hands-on training

111
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:25,830
And the same week, according to Data Center Dynamics

112
00:04:25,880 --> 00:04:30,930
HUMAIN raised $667 million to expand AI infrastructure

113
00:04:30,980 --> 00:04:32,430
in Saudi Arabia

114
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:34,750
So money and skills are moving together: any

115
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:37,670
data center, however big, needs people to build

116
00:04:37,720 --> 00:04:38,590
it and run it

117
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:40,170
Who's going to feel this here?

118
00:04:40,220 --> 00:04:41,250
Companies that are hiring

119
00:04:41,300 --> 00:04:44,710
Skills programs shape who'll be ready to work with AI

120
00:04:44,760 --> 00:04:46,710
And the question we're watching: how much of

121
00:04:46,760 --> 00:04:49,210
this money will build capabilities here, not

122
00:04:49,260 --> 00:04:50,137
just over there?

123
00:04:50,187 --> 00:04:53,190
On to models, and the biggest financial story

124
00:04:53,240 --> 00:04:54,670
of the week

125
00:04:54,720 --> 00:04:57,170
Anthropic, the company behind the Claude model

126
00:04:57,220 --> 00:05:00,530
went public this week, in the largest IPO in history

127
00:05:00,580 --> 00:05:02,410
According to The National, the shares were

128
00:05:02,460 --> 00:05:05,070
priced in the IPO at $83

129
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:07,030
And on the first day of trading, according

130
00:05:07,080 --> 00:05:10,850
to CNBC, the stock closed up 17%

131
00:05:10,900 --> 00:05:12,450
But it didn't last

132
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:15,210
On Friday, according to the BBC, the shares

133
00:05:15,260 --> 00:05:18,870
fell back, at one point by more than 12%, to

134
00:05:18,920 --> 00:05:22,230
about $85, close to the IPO price

135
00:05:22,280 --> 00:05:25,490
They closed the day down 8.5%, giving back

136
00:05:25,540 --> 00:05:27,630
half of Thursday's gains

137
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:30,370
According to WIRED, it now has around $200

138
00:05:30,420 --> 00:05:32,310
billion in the bank

139
00:05:32,360 --> 00:05:34,670
That's more than every big tech company except

140
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:37,050
Alphabet, Google's parent company

141
00:05:37,100 --> 00:05:40,070
Last quarter, its sales were $17 billion

142
00:05:40,120 --> 00:05:43,270
That's an increase of more than 1,100% over

143
00:05:43,320 --> 00:05:44,510
the previous year

144
00:05:44,560 --> 00:05:46,190
But there's another side to the story

145
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:48,070
The company has committed to pay suppliers

146
00:05:48,120 --> 00:05:50,410
more than $900 billion

147
00:05:50,460 --> 00:05:53,190
That's over 19 years, and most of it goes to

148
00:05:53,240 --> 00:05:55,210
data centers that run Claude

149
00:05:55,260 --> 00:05:57,950
And according to WIRED, American voters rejected

150
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,490
data centers in most local votes this month

151
00:06:00,540 --> 00:06:02,270
and protests continue

152
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:04,190
There's even a group of residents in West Virginia

153
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:07,230
fighting a data center still being built for Anthropic

154
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:10,450
And Dario Amodei, the CEO, said safeguards

155
00:06:10,500 --> 00:06:12,850
are what set the pace of model releases

156
00:06:12,900 --> 00:06:14,890
He also said the company delayed a new model

157
00:06:14,940 --> 00:06:18,210
this year to protect people, even if it lost sales

158
00:06:18,260 --> 00:06:21,070
CNBC sees the IPO as a test for all the money

159
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:22,770
flowing into AI startups

160
00:06:22,820 --> 00:06:26,890
And the figure here is more than $350 billion

161
00:06:26,940 --> 00:06:29,070
The founder of investment firm Altimeter said

162
00:06:29,120 --> 00:06:32,610
he plans to invest $5 billion, that's five

163
00:06:32,660 --> 00:06:34,630
times the largest investment he's ever made

164
00:06:34,680 --> 00:06:36,010
in an IPO

165
00:06:36,060 --> 00:06:38,790
Chip companies, including NVIDIA, are investors

166
00:06:38,840 --> 00:06:41,210
too, along with Google and Microsoft

167
00:06:41,260 --> 00:06:43,810
And here's an angle that matters for our region

168
00:06:43,860 --> 00:06:46,850
According to AGBI, Gulf funds made big paper

169
00:06:46,900 --> 00:06:48,630
gains from this listing

170
00:06:48,680 --> 00:06:51,770
Qatar's QIA and the UAE's MGX came in during

171
00:06:51,820 --> 00:06:56,190
the February round, when the company was worth $380 billion

172
00:06:56,240 --> 00:06:58,870
And Global SWF estimates Gulf investors put

173
00:06:58,920 --> 00:07:00,550
in about $8 billion

174
00:07:00,600 --> 00:07:02,210
It's not only the funds

175
00:07:02,260 --> 00:07:05,250
According to Forbes Middle East, Hussain Sajwani

176
00:07:05,300 --> 00:07:08,230
the founder of DAMAC, bought shares in 2024

177
00:07:08,280 --> 00:07:10,630
for $50 million

178
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:12,650
Forbes Middle East valued his stake at the

179
00:07:12,700 --> 00:07:15,850
listing at about $1.9 billion

180
00:07:15,900 --> 00:07:18,910
Remember, these are paper gains, and the funds'

181
00:07:18,960 --> 00:07:20,870
full holdings aren't disclosed

182
00:07:20,920 --> 00:07:23,090
So, what does this mean for a company here

183
00:07:23,140 --> 00:07:24,290
that uses Claude?

184
00:07:24,340 --> 00:07:26,770
A listed company publishes its numbers, so

185
00:07:26,820 --> 00:07:29,290
customers get a clearer view of where it stands

186
00:07:29,340 --> 00:07:31,410
But the data center commitments determine how

187
00:07:31,460 --> 00:07:33,990
much capacity will be available tomorrow, and

188
00:07:34,040 --> 00:07:36,410
according to WIRED, any construction delay

189
00:07:36,460 --> 00:07:39,610
could make it harder to train more powerful models

190
00:07:39,660 --> 00:07:42,470
So, the second story in models?

191
00:07:42,520 --> 00:07:43,730
A price war

192
00:07:43,780 --> 00:07:46,010
Google's DeepMind lab released a new model

193
00:07:46,060 --> 00:07:48,370
It's called Neon, and it's the mid-tier model

194
00:07:48,420 --> 00:07:50,130
in the Gemini 4 family

195
00:07:50,180 --> 00:07:52,310
It's a model that thinks before it answers

196
00:07:52,360 --> 00:07:54,790
built for coding agents and knowledge work

197
00:07:54,840 --> 00:07:56,730
Pricing is per million tokens

198
00:07:56,780 --> 00:07:59,730
$3 for input, and $15 for output

199
00:07:59,780 --> 00:08:02,130
And the context window is 1 million tokens

200
00:08:02,180 --> 00:08:05,510
so it can read very long documents in one go

201
00:08:05,560 --> 00:08:07,910
Google says the cost per task on Neon is lower

202
00:08:07,960 --> 00:08:10,610
than competitors' by 50 to 80%

203
00:08:10,660 --> 00:08:13,610
But note: these are Google's own figures, not

204
00:08:13,660 --> 00:08:15,390
from an independent test

205
00:08:15,440 --> 00:08:17,490
And even in their own table, Neon didn't win

206
00:08:17,540 --> 00:08:18,890
every benchmark

207
00:08:18,940 --> 00:08:21,910
Models from Anthropic and OpenAI beat it on a few

208
00:08:21,960 --> 00:08:24,850
And from China, Alibaba released its most powerful

209
00:08:24,900 --> 00:08:26,690
model yet, called Qwen

210
00:08:26,740 --> 00:08:29,930
The model has 2.4 trillion parameters

211
00:08:29,980 --> 00:08:33,710
But for each token, it only runs 71 billion

212
00:08:33,760 --> 00:08:36,010
Picture a company with lots of experts, but

213
00:08:36,060 --> 00:08:38,290
each question goes to just a few of them

214
00:08:38,340 --> 00:08:39,830
That's how it saves compute

215
00:08:39,880 --> 00:08:42,330
And according to a report on KuCoin, it's the

216
00:08:42,380 --> 00:08:44,630
top-ranked Chinese model on the agent leaderboard

217
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:48,190
at Arena, which rates models through real conversations

218
00:08:48,240 --> 00:08:50,470
What's the lesson for our companies?

219
00:08:50,520 --> 00:08:53,050
The price per token isn't the whole story

220
00:08:53,100 --> 00:08:55,330
The more accurate comparison now is how much

221
00:08:55,380 --> 00:08:57,050
the whole task costs

222
00:08:57,100 --> 00:08:59,450
And a strong Chinese option widens the choices

223
00:08:59,500 --> 00:09:02,450
here, but it raises governance questions: where

224
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:05,670
does the data go, and under which laws?

225
00:09:05,720 --> 00:09:09,130
Now let's move to agents, and back to Microsoft

226
00:09:09,180 --> 00:09:11,830
At its annual Ignite conference, Nadella announced

227
00:09:11,880 --> 00:09:14,210
shared workspaces inside Copilot

228
00:09:14,260 --> 00:09:16,450
People and agents work on the same document

229
00:09:16,500 --> 00:09:18,590
The agent writes a section, you review and

230
00:09:18,640 --> 00:09:21,390
comment, and the agent picks up the comment

231
00:09:21,440 --> 00:09:24,570
Microsoft also announced an agent called Autopilot

232
00:09:24,620 --> 00:09:27,410
According to Microsoft, it monitors Teams channels

233
00:09:27,460 --> 00:09:30,410
follows up on emails and runs a project, without

234
00:09:30,460 --> 00:09:31,770
anyone asking it to

235
00:09:31,820 --> 00:09:34,510
And here's the detail that matters to tech teams

236
00:09:34,560 --> 00:09:37,190
Every agent gets its own identity in Microsoft's

237
00:09:37,240 --> 00:09:40,350
identity system, and a named human manager

238
00:09:40,400 --> 00:09:42,730
Its sign-in and every action it takes are logged

239
00:09:42,780 --> 00:09:45,310
under its name, so there's a clear audit trail

240
00:09:45,360 --> 00:09:47,610
and the tech team can shut it down

241
00:09:47,660 --> 00:09:50,070
But ZDNET cautioned that teams should verify

242
00:09:50,120 --> 00:09:52,090
the claim about running meetings on its own

243
00:09:52,140 --> 00:09:53,470
for themselves

244
00:09:53,520 --> 00:09:56,030
And through a new partnership, data from Salesforce

245
00:09:56,080 --> 00:09:58,490
the well-known customer relationship management

246
00:09:58,540 --> 00:10:02,150
system, now flows into Copilot, in preview

247
00:10:02,200 --> 00:10:04,850
Why does this matter for us here in the region?

248
00:10:04,900 --> 00:10:07,170
Because the agent now has an identity, almost

249
00:10:07,220 --> 00:10:08,430
like an employee

250
00:10:08,480 --> 00:10:10,250
So the question for any organization running

251
00:10:10,300 --> 00:10:13,490
on Microsoft is: who grants the agent its permissions

252
00:10:13,540 --> 00:10:15,410
and who reviews its log?

253
00:10:15,460 --> 00:10:17,790
And after the November outage, putting your

254
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:21,290
agents with the same provider is a question in itself

255
00:10:21,340 --> 00:10:24,410
So, what about when the agent needs to pay?

256
00:10:24,460 --> 00:10:27,910
Last week we covered ChatGPT's new wallet for consumers

257
00:10:27,960 --> 00:10:31,030
And this week, on the enterprise side, AWS

258
00:10:31,080 --> 00:10:33,670
Amazon's cloud service, made a move too

259
00:10:33,720 --> 00:10:36,150
It now lets agents buy software and data from

260
00:10:36,200 --> 00:10:39,410
its marketplace, in dollars, on the cloud bill

261
00:10:39,460 --> 00:10:41,710
According to Amazon itself, there's a spending

262
00:10:41,760 --> 00:10:44,870
cap, and every transaction is logged for auditing

263
00:10:44,920 --> 00:10:46,970
Where does the region stand on this?

264
00:10:47,020 --> 00:10:49,690
Banks, card issuers and online stores here

265
00:10:49,740 --> 00:10:52,130
face a new question: what happens when the

266
00:10:52,180 --> 00:10:53,990
agent is the one paying?

267
00:10:54,040 --> 00:10:56,270
In the world of chips, three headlines point

268
00:10:56,320 --> 00:10:58,390
the same way, and we're covering them carefully

269
00:10:58,440 --> 00:11:00,870
because details are still thin

